 ## 2026-08-22
 
+### 2325 — Goal-mode launch preparation cannot live behind any plugin method
+- DECISION: The claude port carries goal mode's ARGV half and explicitly leaves its PREPARATION half out of the plan surface. `pkg/agentic/systems/claude/goal.go` states the whole of what the source does there.
+- FINDING: `PrepareClaudeGoalLaunch` (source, `spawn/claude_goal.go`) does four things before construction: validates the board goal contract, gates on Claude Code >= 2.1.139 by running `claude --version`, probes `/goal` capability by running `claude -p --output-format json /goal`, and decides a session action (`native_goal_bound` / `binding_retained` / `successor_required`). Steps two and three START PROCESSES. `BuildPlan` calls EVERY `System` method to build a DRY RUN, so preparation behind `ResolveBinary`, `Argv` or `Stdin` would make a dry run execute the harness twice — the exact drift this repo's dry-run-as-a-mode design exists to prevent. Step four is not a plan fact at all: it decides about a session that already exists, which the source itself records by making `ClaudeGoalBinding.Rollback` a documented no-op.
+- DECISION: One piece of preparation IS carried across — the refusal of a goal whose provider condition is empty. Without it a goal-bound launch ships the literal argument `/goal ` and the child is told it is bound to nothing, with no error anywhere. A validating gate must not get weaker across a move; this repo has recorded that failure once already, on `CompositionServer.BearerTokenEnvVar`.
+- NOTE: Home for the rest is the launch/session plane when that is ported, in the position `PreflightClaudeGoalLaunch` holds in the source's command layer. This task invents no home for it.
+- SCOPE: `pkg/agentic/systems/claude/goal.go`, `args.go`, `args_test.go`.
+- STATUS: TASK-260822-3u97y3 handed to review, uncommitted for the CR snapshot.
+
+### 2320 — The claude env contract is ONE key, and the codex plugin is the trap
+- FINDING: A claude child in the source inherits the whole `CODEX_*` family, both app-server/session-manager URLs, the credentials their `*_AUTH_TOKEN_ENV` pointers name, and `TASK_BOARD_SESSION_ID`. `buildClaudeCommand` is `withSpawnEnv(filterEnv(os.Environ(), "CLAUDECODE"), cfg)` — one exact key. `filterCodexRuntimeEnv` is a CODEX child's filter; `filterQwenRuntimeEnv` is that PLUS `CLAUDECODE` for a qwen child. Claude's is neither.
+- NOTE: The spawn brief for this task asserted the opposite ("the codex family is stripped from claude children") and named the goldens as the authority. The goldens settle it: `env_removed` for both claude cases is `CLAUDECODE` plus the four run-context keys `withSpawnEnv` replaces, and every seeded `CODEX_*` key survives. Read the code and the fixture, not the brief.
+- FINDING: This is `BUG-260819-3qn52o` seen from the other side, and the general shape is what matters: every filter names the OTHER runtimes it knew about when it was written, so a seventh runtime opens a leak in both directions by existing. Not fixable one plugin at a time, and not fixable inside a parity port whose acceptance is byte-identical launch surfaces.
+- NOTE: Two claims no golden can hold, so both got their own test: the codex family SURVIVING (`TestTheClaudeChildKeepsTheCodexFamily` — the goldens show it, but only as an absence from `env_removed`), and PATH not being sanitized (the capture excludes PATH from its diff entirely; the fixtures README names the residual).
+- DECISION: The strip is `agentic.SetEnvValue(env, key, "")` rather than a local loop. Whole-key matching already has one implementation in this module and a second would agree until the day one was widened.
+- SCOPE: `pkg/agentic/systems/claude/env.go`, `env_test.go`, `parity_negatives_test.go`.
+- STATUS: TASK-260822-3u97y3 handed to review, uncommitted for the CR snapshot.
+
+### 2312 — A bare-name argv allowlist stops working the moment a second plugin lands
+- ROOT CAUSE: The codex argv guard's allowlist was keyed by FUNCTION NAME. The claude plugin names its construction site `Args` too — as every plugin will — so a bare-name allowlist exempts every `Args` in the module from every plugin's guard. The codex guard would have silently lost the ability to report a second codex construction named `Args` one directory over, and nothing would have failed.
+- FIX: Allowlist keys are `<module-relative file>::<name>` (`internal/argvguard.AllowlistKey`). Both plugins' guards carry a mutant planting the identical construction under a different filename with the allowlisted name; both must report it.
+- DECISION: The scanner itself moved to `internal/argvguard`, shared, and the module-source walk to `internal/gosources`, shared with `pkg/agentic`'s single-source guard. Two scanners with the same threshold today are two definitions of the rule tomorrow, and both report clean when they disagree — which is the failure the rule exists to prevent. Each plugin now supplies only what is genuinely its own: the signature literals and the allowlist.
+- FINDING: A signature literal has to be checked against the OTHER agents in the source before it is adopted. `--dangerously-skip-permissions` is claude's most characteristic flag AND is spelled by `buildAgyArgs` (`spawn.go:1185`); `--output-format` is spelled by qwen and agy. Including either would make claude's guard fire on a legitimate sibling plugin the day one is ported, and a guard that reports honest code is a guard somebody deletes. Both are excluded, the resulting residual (a copy of the unconditional prompt-mode flags alone) is named on `claudeArgvSignature`, and `TestTheDeclaredResidualStaysOpen` holds the comment to the code.
+- SCOPE: `internal/argvguard/`, `internal/gosources/`, `internal/launchenv/` (new), `pkg/agentic/systems/{codex,claude}/argvguard_test.go`, `pkg/agentic/singlesource_guard_test.go`, `pkg/agentic/singlesource_scanscope_test.go`.
+- STATUS: TASK-260822-3u97y3 handed to review, uncommitted for the CR snapshot.
+
+### 2305 — The goal predicate is rendered by the board, so a plugin can only carry it
+- DECISION: `agentic.Goal` gained `ProviderCondition`. The claude adapter reads `goal.ProviderCondition` and splices `/goal <condition>` into argv; the TEXT is rendered by `pkgboard.RenderGoalProviderCondition` and the board validates a stored contract against a RE-render.
+- FINDING: So a plugin that rendered the predicate itself would be a second renderer for a fact the board owns, producing a directive the board rejects as tampered. Without the field the only alternatives were that second renderer or dropping the binding entirely. It is deliberately NOT `Objective`: the objective is operator intent and reaches claude's argv and environment nowhere at all.
+- NOTE: The claude parity test spells the renderer's FORMAT STRING and `fmt.Sprintf`s it rather than copying the rendered text out of the fixture it compares to. A case that reads its expected value out of its own golden proves only that JSON round-trips.
+- SCOPE: `pkg/agentic/system.go`, `pkg/agentic/systems/claude/goal.go`, `parity_test.go`.
+- STATUS: TASK-260822-3u97y3 handed to review, uncommitted for the CR snapshot.
+
 ### 2140 — Binary resolution that reads the process environment cannot be dry-run-proven
 - DECISION: The codex plugin resolves its binary from `LaunchRequest.Env`, not from `os.Getenv`/`exec.LookPath`. `pkg/agentic/systems/codex/binary.go`.
 - NOTE: The source read the ambient process env for both `CODEX_MANAGED_PACKAGE_ROOT` and `PATH`. Under the plugin contract that is unprovable: `System` promises a plugin reaches for no ambient state and that a dry run reports the same target a real launch would, and neither claim survives a process-global. Reading the handed environment also makes all three resolution paths testable in parallel against stub layouts with no `t.Setenv` anywhere.
